Sweep the block's framing, and isolate the mapping from the registration

## What has been measured with r72

Registration appended to the packet -the Pixel 3a's method- **is rejected by
the DSP**:

    registering topologies: 1052 bytes ... (adsp ready)
    in-band registration: 1052 bytes of topologies, packet of 1088
    the DSP refuses to register the topologies: 2      <- ADSP_EBADPARAM

And it rejects it **just the same** with surya's genuine calibration (1052
bytes) as with the Pixel 4a's (1316 bytes). It was tried by changing the file
on the fly, one after the other.

**That rules out the content as the cause.** The wrong file was a real bug and
it is fixed, but it was not the reason for the rejection. What is left is the
**framing**: how the block is presented to the DSP.

## What this patch does

### 1. The framing, adjustable without recompiling

The block lives in the ACDB preceded by a u32 with its size. The Pixel 3a skips
that u32 and sends the rest. Now it can be swept:

    /sys/module/q6core/parameters/topo_offset   bytes skipped         [4]
    /sys/module/q6core/parameters/topo_delta    added to the size     [0]

Combinations that make sense: `4 0` (like the Pixel 3a, rejected), `0 +4` (send
the u32 as well and count it), `0 0`, `4 -4`. All of them can be tried without
a call and without rebooting.

### 2. The mapping token

    /sys/module/q6core/parameters/mapeo_token    [0]

q6asm -which maps memory on this very phone without breaking a sweat- fills in
the token of the APR packet; we were leaving it at zero. It is the last cheap
difference that was left and it was never tried.

### 3. A trigger that tests ONLY the mapping

    echo 1 > /sys/bus/aprbus/devices/aprsvc:service:4:3/probar_mapeo

It reserves 4 KiB, maps them, unmaps them and traces. Without registering
anything afterwards.

It exists to separate two things that until now went together: **whether what
brings the phone down is the mapping itself, or the registration command that
came after it.** Without separating them there is no way to know which one to
fix, and each attempt cost a blind reboot.

DANGER: if the mapping is what brings it down, this reboots the phone. But
rebooting costs 90 seconds and **no call**, so it is worth it.

## Why this matters more than it looks

In Qualcomm's downstream, `AVCS_CMD_REGISTER_TOPOLOGIES` **always** goes
through shared memory; never appended to the packet. The Pixel 3a patch sends
it appended, but **does not look at the reply**: it does `return
apr_send_pkt(...)` and checks nothing. So it could be being rejected there too
without anyone having noticed.

If sweeping the framing does not fix it, the conclusion is that this DSP
demands shared memory, and then the problem to solve is no longer the
registration: it is **why the mapping brings the phone down**. That is what
point 3 is for.

